```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  const regex = /([a-z]+(\s+(jja[m-z]{2,}|de|li|qu|re|un)|[a-z]+[-']?[a-z]+)\b)[^\s,.]+\s+([a-z]+(\s+(jja[m-z]{2,}|de|li|qu|re|un)|[a-z]+[-']?[a-z]+)\b)/g;
  let match, newSentence = sentence;

  while ((match = regex.exec(sentence)) !== null) {
    const [_, firstAdj, secondAdj] = match;
    newSentence = newSentence.replace(new RegExp(`(${firstAdj})\\s+(${secondAdj})`, 'g'), `${secondAdj} ${firstAdj}`);
  }

  return newSentence;
}

console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Beautiful green trees are in the park")); // "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Red hot chili peppers are delicious")); // "Hot red chili peppers are delicious"
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder("Happy blue elephants dance in the jungle")); // "Blue happy elephants dance in the jungle"
```

This function utilizes a regular expression to find pairs of adjacent adjectives in a sentence. It then replaces those pairs with their reversed order using a `RegExp`. The function maintains the original sentence structure while switching the order of the adjectives.

