 To create a React utility for removing widows, you can write a custom hook that utilizes the `react-big-calendar` library's `localizer` and `formatSpacing` functions. This approach will help you adjust the last line of a text in a calendar event to prevent unwanted single words from appearing on the next line, also known as widows.

First, make sure you have installed `react-big-calendar`:

```bash
npm install react-big-calendar
```

Next, create a new file called `removeWidows.js` in your project's `utils` folder:

```javascript
import moment from 'moment';
import { localizer, formatSpacing } from 'react-big-calendar';

const createRemoveWidowsLocalizer = () =>
  localizer((date) => {
    const formattedDate = moment(date).format('YYYY-MM-DD');
    return { date, label: formattedDate };
  });

const removeWidows = (content) => {
  if (!content || typeof content !== 'string') return content;

  const lines = content.split('\n').filter((line) => line.length > 0);
  const lastLineIndex = lines.length - 1;

  if (lastLineIndex < 0) return content;

  const lastLine = lines[lastLineIndex];
  const words = lastLine.trim().split(' ');
  const maxWidth = 65; // Define your maximum width here

  if (words.length > 0 && words[words.length - 1].length > maxWidth) {
    const lastWord = words.pop();
    const remainingContent = content.substring(0, content.lastIndexOf('\n', content.lastIndexOf(lastWord) - 1));
    const newLastLine = words.join(' ') + '.'; // Adding a period to indicate truncation
    const newContent = `${remainingContent}\n${newLastLine}`;

    return newContent;
  }

  return content;
};

export const BigCalendarLocalizer = createRemoveWidowsLocalizer();
export default removeWidows;
```

