response:
 To update the state with a new property for a specific registration object in your array, you can use the `setState` function with a callback that merges the current state with the updated registration object. Here's how you can modify your `checkMNPStatusAPI` function to achieve this:

```javascript
checkMNPStatusAPI(index, phoneNo, portInStatus) {
  console.log("mnpPhoneNo: " + phoneNo);

  const updatedRegistrations = [...this.state.registrations]; // Create a copy of the current state
  updatedRegistrations[index] = {
    ...updatedRegistrations[index], // Spread the existing registration object
    mnpStatus: portInStatus, // Add the new property with the provided value
  };

  this.setState(prevState => ({ // Use a callback to merge the updated state with the existing one
    registrations: prevState.registrations.map((registration, i) => (i === index ? updatedRegistrations[index] : registration)), // Replace the specific registration object in the state
  }));
}
```

